Exactly. That’s the engine.

Nicolas Cage is living in a mythic action-thriller reality where every receipt is prophecy, every churro is evidence, every employee is potentially compromised, and every completely ordinary object is one camera push-in away from revealing the fate of civilization.

Meanwhile everybody else is trapped in regular physics going:

“Sir, that is a coupon.”

And the comedy comes from the mismatch never resolving. Cage does not wink at the audience. He is 100% sincere. The stranger the world gets, the more professionally he responds to it.

Jessica’s job is basically to be the human checksum:

“Nicolas, you cannot interrogate a vending machine.”

“THEN WHY DID IT TAKE MY DOLLAR?”

And Tiny Cage is even better because instead of correcting him, he validates the insane ontology:

Jessica: “There is no conspiracy.”

Tiny Cage, examining a Dorito under a flashlight: “Negative. We’re already inside it.”

That gives us a nice reusable comedy pattern too: one character inhabits Genre A with absolute conviction while everyone else remains in Genre B. Don Quixote is heroic romance inside mundane reality. Leslie Nielsen plays deadly serious inside absurdity. Cage here is National Treasure inside a Costco.

The crucial bit is that reality occasionally rewards him just enough to make everyone else question themselves.

“THE CHURRO IS THE KEY.”

“That is unbelievably stupid.”

Ancient door opens.

Jessica: “…I fucking hate when you do this.”